Description
-----------

A collection of various multi-player card games; so far they are:

  - Røvhult (the Danish word for arsehole): Obviously I learned it from Danes,
    but it is also known internationally (as Shithead or Asshole).

  - Twopart: A game I learned from some Norwegians. They probably mentioned
    its name, though I was not able to remember it (you have no idea how long
    it took me to learn the word "Røvhult" - not to mention the pronunciation).
    So the name simply describes the game.

  - Buraco: A popular game in Venezuela; somewhat similar to Canasta.

  - Machiavelli: Add your cards to the ones on the table without getting
    (too) confused while moving them around.

  - Sgt. Mayor: Every player must make a certain number of tricks. Making more
    is rewarded and making fewer is punished in the next round.

  - Jabberwocky: Bid the number of tricks you think you are going to win.

  - Hearts: The game included in various versions of Windows.


Card decks
----------

Card decks are not included. The build configuration tries to find out where
the GNOME card decks (from GNOME Games), the KDE card decks (from KDE Games)
or the cardpics (see https://www.nongnu.org/cardpics/) are installed and uses
those.


Installation
------------

See the file INSTALL.


Documentation
-------------

The documentation is in the doc directory, in HTML format. If it is properly
installed (with "meson install"; see also the file INSTALL) or its path is
specified either in the INI file (Helpdir) or with the option --dir-help, it
can be accessed from within the program (by pressing F1 or via Help -
Contents).


How to report bugs and/or send patches
--------------------------------------

Bug reports and patches should be sent to the e-mail address of the author
(pelotudo.gringo@gmail.com).

For bugs, please include:

  - The version of the package. In case you don't know it: It is shown in the
    title of the About box.
  - The version of the libYGP library used.
  - How to reproduce the bug.
  - In case of a crash, a stack trace (e.g. from your system log or a
    debugger), which might help in locating the bug.
  - Anything else you think might be helpful.

Please note that I don't (can't) check my mail daily, so please be patient.
